Here's a clear comparison between RabbitMQ and ActiveMQ based on important interview aspects.

Note:
- **ActiveMQ Classic (5.x)** and **ActiveMQ Artemis** are different products.
- Artemis is the newer/faster ActiveMQ line; many “ActiveMQ is slow/limited” points apply more to Classic.
- RabbitMQ is usually compared with Classic in older notes; modern answers should mention Artemis.


🔷 Overview
| Feature       | **RabbitMQ**                                 | **ActiveMQ**                                      |
| ------------- | -------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------- |
| Type          | Message Broker (AMQP-first)                  | Message Broker (JMS-first; OpenWire / Artemis)    |
| Written In    | Erlang                                       | Java                                              |
| Maintained By | Broadcom (formerly VMware / Pivotal)         | Apache Software Foundation                        |


🔷 Protocol Support
| Feature          | RabbitMQ                         | ActiveMQ / Artemis                 |
| ---------------- | -------------------------------- | ---------------------------------- |
| Primary model    | AMQP                             | JMS API + native protocols         |
| Other protocols  | MQTT, STOMP, HTTP, WebSockets    | STOMP, MQTT, AMQP, HTTP, OpenWire  |

Interview tip:
JMS is an **API** (Java Message Service). OpenWire/AMQP/STOMP are **wire protocols**.


🔷 Performance
| Feature    | RabbitMQ                              | ActiveMQ Classic           | ActiveMQ Artemis      |
| ---------- | ------------------------------------- | -------------------------- | --------------------- |
| Throughput | High (very strong for many workloads) | Moderate                   | High                  |
| Latency    | Low                                   | Usually higher than Rabbit | Competitive / better  |

Don’t memorize “Rabbit always wins.” Depends on workload, persistence, consumers, and broker tuning.


🔷 Message Delivery
| Feature          | RabbitMQ                                      | ActiveMQ                         |
| ---------------- | --------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------- |
| Acknowledgements | Yes (manual + auto)                           | Yes                              |
| Message Ordering | Per queue (with competing-consumer caveats)   | Per queue / destination          |
| Durability       | Durable queue + persistent message (mode=2)   | Persistent destinations/messages |

Important (RabbitMQ nuance you already learned):
Durable queue alone is not enough — message persistence also matters.


🔷 Clustering & High Availability
| Feature            | RabbitMQ                                               | ActiveMQ                                      |
| ------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------ | --------------------------------------------- |
| Clustering         | Native cluster                                         | Network of brokers / cluster options          |
| Queue ownership    | Queue has a leader/master node (not “shared everywhere”)| Similar idea depending on setup               |
| High Availability  | Prefer **Quorum Queues** (modern). Mirrored queues are deprecated | Classic: Master/Slave etc. Artemis: replication / HA |
| Horizontal Scaling | Good with queues + consumers                           | Classic more limited; Artemis much better     |

❌ Old wrong line to avoid:
“RabbitMQ clustering = queues shared across all nodes.”
✅ Correct:
Cluster connects nodes; for HA of queue data use quorum queues (or legacy mirrors).


🔷 Routing Power (big RabbitMQ strength)
| Feature            | RabbitMQ                                      | ActiveMQ                          |
| ------------------ | --------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------- |
| Exchange types     | Direct, Topic, Fanout, Headers                | Topics / selectors / destinations |
| Complex routing    | Excellent (routing keys + bindings)           | Possible, but different model     |
| Pub/Sub + work queues| Very natural                                | Natural via JMS topics/queues     |


🔷 Tooling & Monitoring
| Feature   | RabbitMQ                      | ActiveMQ                |
| --------- | ----------------------------- | ----------------------- |
| Web UI    | Strong built-in management UI | Available (less modern for Classic) |
| CLI Tools | Yes                           | Yes                     |


🔷 Use Cases
| Prefer RabbitMQ                         | Prefer ActiveMQ (often Classic / JMS shops)      |
| --------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------ |
| Complex routing / workflows             | Strong JMS / Java EE legacy integration          |
| Microservices + polyglot clients        | Existing enterprise ActiveMQ landscape           |
| Cloud-native / multi-protocol messaging | Teams standardized on JMS already                |
| Spring Boot with AMQP                   | Spring JMS + ActiveMQ                            |

Correction vs older notes:
Spring Boot works great with **both** RabbitMQ and ActiveMQ.
“Spring apps = ActiveMQ only” is misleading.


🔷 When to Use What?
🔹 Use RabbitMQ if:
- You want flexible routing (exchanges/bindings/routing keys)
- Microservices / polyglot systems
- You prefer AMQP + strong management UI
- You need modern HA with quorum queues

🔹 Use ActiveMQ if:
- You are deep in JMS / Java EE style messaging
- Company already runs ActiveMQ
- Legacy integrations expect JMS destinations
- For new ActiveMQ projects, prefer **Artemis** over Classic


🆚 Final Verdict (Quick Interview Summary)
| Category              | Typical edge                         |
| --------------------- | ------------------------------------ |
| Protocol flexibility  | RabbitMQ                             |
| JMS / Java EE fit     | ActiveMQ                             |
| Complex routing       | RabbitMQ                             |
| Modern HA / scaling   | RabbitMQ or Artemis (not old Classic)|
| Existing JMS codebase | ActiveMQ                             |
| Cloud-native default today | Often RabbitMQ (or Kafka for event log) |


Interview one-liners:
- RabbitMQ = great routing + AMQP broker for microservices.
- ActiveMQ = Java/JMS-friendly broker; use Artemis for modern ActiveMQ.
- Kafka is different category (event log / replay), not a drop-in ActiveMQ replacement.
